%description:

RFC 792: if the destination host cannot deliver the datagram because the indicated port is not
active, the destination host may send a destination unreachable message, code 3, to the
source. RFC 768: the destination port selects the receiver; a port that no program opened
selects none.

Checks RFC792-DU-3 (may). Also covers RFC768-HDR-2 (the closed-port half) from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc768/catalog.md (the RFC792 catalog holds RFC792-DU-3 itself).
The English procedure and the mockup are in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/udp/checks/port-unreachable.md#port-unreachable; the program steps below map one
to one onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup: host A = hostA, host B = hostB;
link 1 = hostA-hostB. Host B opens no application port at all (numApps = 0), so port 6000 is
certainly closed.

The ICMP step (observation 3) asserts a "may" clause of RFC 792, the same status the
Rfc791TtlExpiry.test ICMP step already carries: a silent discard also conforms to the RFCs. If
this step fails on some implementation, record an implementation gap against the "may" clause,
not a specification violation (see doc/project/evidence/protocol/udp/checks/port-unreachable.md#port-unreachable,
Notes). Verified in Udp.cc: Udp::processUndeliverablePacket() is reached when
findSocketForUnicastPacket() finds no match; it emits packetDroppedSignal (reason
NO_PORT_FOUND) on the packet before it builds the Icmpv4SendErrorReq (type 3 /
ICMP_DESTINATION_UNREACHABLE, code 3 / ICMP_DU_PORT_UNREACHABLE) and dispatches it to ICMP.
StandardHost's IPv4 stack instantiates an `icmp: Icmp` submodule unconditionally
(Ipv4NetworkLayer.ned), so host A's own stack is not what is under test here -- host B is.

Deviation on observation 2's filter: a plain .packet("udp.destPort == 6000") does not match
here, even though the UDP header is genuinely still present on the packet at that point (the
front offset is restored before the emit, in processUDPPacket(), so peekAtFront<UdpHeader>()
in processUndeliverablePacket() succeeds). The reason is upstream of the header itself:
processUDPPacket() unconditionally calls udpPacket->removeTag<PacketProtocolTag>() as its very
first action, for every packet it receives, before it even pops the header. The tester's
.packet(expr) dissects via PacketDissector::dissectPacket(Packet*) (PacketField.cc), which
looks up that same PacketProtocolTag to pick which protocol's dissector to start with
(PacketDissector.cc); with the tag gone, it falls back to DefaultProtocolDissector (registered
for protocol == nullptr), which does not attribute the UDP header chunk to protocol "udp" --
confirmed with a temporary diagnostic .match(lambda) that both a protocol-name lookup
(evalPacketField(..., "udp.destPort")) and a chunk-class lookup
(evalPacketField(..., "UdpHeader.destPort")) throw "protocol '...' not present in packet" for
this exact event, even though the event itself does arrive (byteLength 108, confirmed by the
same diagnostic) -- so neither PacketFilter path can attribute this chunk once the tag is gone.
Ipv4's own drop paths (e.g. the TTL-expiry one in Ipv4.cc) never strip this tag, so
Rfc791TtlExpiry.test's equivalent ipv4.identification filter on a packetDropped signal never
hits this. The fix confirms the same content honestly through a property that does not depend
on dissection at all -- the packet's byte length, 108 (8-octet header + 100 octets of data),
the same tag-independent technique this suite already uses for hostB.udp's packetSentToUpper
handoffs (see Rfc768DatagramDelivery.test and Rfc768Checksum.test) -- rather than through a
re-dissected port field.

Observation 4 needs no packet filter: the only datagram host B's UDP ever sees in this
scenario is the one to the closed port 6000, so asserting that packetSentToUpper never fires at
all is equivalent to asserting that no program is ever handed anything.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc768_port_unreachable")
        // Expected observation 1: link 1 shows the datagram to port 6000 from port 4000 --
        // confirms the stimulus.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("udp.srcPort == 4000 && udp.destPort == 6000")
                  .describe("the datagram on link 1 to port 6000 from port 4000")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Expected observation 2: host B's UDP discards the datagram -- a discard for a port
        // without a program is recorded at host B (RFC768-HDR-2, the closed-port half). This
        // is in time, before the ICMP report is built, so it does not anchor too late. Filtered
        // by size, not by a re-dissected udp.destPort -- see the %description deviation note.
        .once(on("hostB.udp").signal("packetDropped")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return c.event.packet->getByteLength() == 108; // 8-octet UDP header + 100 octets of data
                  })
                  .describe("host B's UDP discard of the 108-octet datagram to the closed port 6000")
                  .within(0.1))
        // Expected observation 3: host A receives an ICMP destination unreachable message,
        // type 3, code 3 (RFC792-DU-3).
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .packet("icmpv4.type == 3 && icmpv4.code == 3") // the dissector protocol name is icmpv4
                  .describe("the ICMP destination unreachable (port) message received at host A")
                  .within(0.1))
        // Expected observation 4: from then on, host B's UDP hands nothing upward -- no
        // program ever receives the datagram.
        .never(on("hostB.udp").signal("packetSentToUpper")
                  .describe("any datagram handed up to a program at host B")
                  .within(0.3));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc768_port_unreachable compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/udp/checks/port-unreachable.md#port-unreachable: hostA -- hostB.
network Rfc768PortUnreachableNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> hostB.ethg++; // link 1
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc768PortUnreachableNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Host A: 100 octets from port 4000 to host B's port 6000.
*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.hostA.app[0].destAddresses = "hostB"
*.hostA.app[0].destPort = 6000
*.hostA.app[0].localPort = 4000
*.hostA.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.hostA.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.hostA.app[0].sendInterval = 1s

# Host B: no application at all -- port 6000 (and every other port) is closed.
*.hostB.numApps = 0

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc768_port_unreachable: PASS
